    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-10716",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45a^4}}{\\sqrt{5}}  = \\sqrt{\\dfrac {45a^4} {5} }= \\sqrt{9a^4}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3{a}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ |3{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |{a}^{2}| & = \\\\ 3\\cdot {a}^{2} & = \\\\ 3{a}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
